.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Retrieves a shell variable from the bash\-server session associated with
the client connection \f[B]c\f[R].
On success, the \f[B]info\f[R] structure is populated with the
variable\(cqs name, value, and any attribute flags (such as
\f[CR]exported\f[R], \f[CR]readonly\f[R], \f[CR]integer\f[R], etc.).
.PP
The function sends a \f[CR]get\f[R] request on the STATE channel
(\f[CR]CHAN_STATE\f[R], channel 2) with namespace \f[CR]\(dqvar\(dq\f[R]
and the given variable name.
The server responds with the variable\(cqs current value and attributes,
which are decoded and stored into the caller\-provided
\f[CR]bc_var_info_t\f[R] structure.
.PP
All strings within \f[B]info\f[R] are freshly allocated.
The caller is responsible for freeing them by calling
\f[CR]bc_var_info_free()\f[R] when they are no longer needed.
Failure to do so will result in memory leaks.
.PP
If the variable does not exist on the server, the function returns
\f[CR]BC_ERR_SERVER\f[R] and the contents of \f[B]info\f[R] are left in
an indeterminate state (no strings are allocated in the error case).
